RELEASE NOTES, c3p0-0.13.0
==========================

This release addresses remaining security issues deriving from c3p0 and its
supporting libraries using Java serialization in constructing and decoding
JNDI javax.naming.Reference objects.

Prior to c3p0-0.12.0 / mchange-commons-java-0.4.0, attackers could craft
javax.naming.Reference objects, which upon dereferencing, would download
and execute malicious code.

c3p0-0.12.0 eliminated the most critical issues, preventing download of
code from arbitrary factoryClassLocation URLs, preventing JNDI lookups
from remote name services, and preventing use of potentially malicious
configuration (initial context environment) that might have been serialized
into references.

However, c3p0's own references still relied upon Java serialization of
nested objects. So, subject to restrictions that ony code on the application's
codebase could be accessed, only using the application's default JNDI config,
it remained possible for deserialization of objects embedded in References
to execute code by, for example, causing a superclass constructor of
whatever is getting deserialized to be invoked.

This is a pretty narrow attack surface. A malicious Reference could only
cause execution through rather restrictive pathways, and only of code
already on the CLASSPATH.

Nevertheless, modern applications have a lot of dependencies, and so a lot
of code on the CLASSPATH for a clever attacker to work with. c3p0's
use of Java serialization in resolving javax.naming.Reference objects could
still play a role in a chain of invocations that might constitute an effective
attack.

c3p0-0.13.0 / mchange-commons-java-0.5.0 eliminate this pathway entirely.
c3p0 and its dependent libraries no longer create or decode javax.naming.Reference
objects containing Java-serialized binary objects. All items in c3p0's references
are now stored in String formats. The complicated and dangerous
machinery of Java serialization is eschewed entirely.

In particular, com.mchange.v2.naming.JavaBeanObjectFactory has been hardened,
so it will never unmarshall Java-serialized BinaryRefAddr components. That
eldrich magic is disabled, unless users actively re-enable it via their own
subclass of that ObjectFactory.

See https://www.mchange.com/projects/c3p0/#configuring_security for
details, particularly documentation of config parameter

   com.mchange.v2.naming.generateSerializedObjectBinaryRefAddr

---

This version also addresses a rare race condition that occurs because
end-of-session was marked (using JDBC 4.3 endRequest()) *after* Connections
were checked in to the pool.

JDBC drivers sometimes handle endRequest() with cleanup operations on the
Connection. If a new client checks out a Connection prior to the endRequest()
call, which was possible because endRequest() was called after the Connection
was returned to the pool, then the new client might see their Statements and
ResultSets cleaned out from underneath them.

c3p0 now ensures that endRequest() is called prior to the return of the
Connection to the pool.

Thanks to Krrish (ota0912 on GitHub) for discovering and analyzing this issue.



